No trial has clearly beaten the starting network. Its 4-seed mean is 0.116, and every result has a seed at or near the 0.068 plateau. Only two seeds broke out: the footprint head r1c1 (0.227) and landing gating r1c3 (0.170). The gaps between these two and the start are within seed noise. Changes that made things worse or did not help:
- fewer PPO epochs (r2c1);
- orthogonal PPO initialisation (r2c3);
- longer horizon, gamma 0.99 (all three trials that used it);
- raw-observation skip (r3c3).
What hurt most is having less optimisation per update or a noisier credit signal. That suggests the agent needs more policy-improvement steps per sample, not more samples. One change is free under the compute budget: n_minibatches 64 doubles the gradient steps without cutting training steps. I test it on both architectures that broke out. As a new architectural idea, I give the footprint head global context (progress and how much is still missing) by broadcasting pooled features back onto the map before scoring.
